Git Merge vs Git Rebase – Explained with a Real-Life Example

Imagine you're working on a feature branch, and your teammate has pushed new updates to the main branch while you were coding.

Now you want to bring those changes into your branch.

You have two choices: Git Merge or Git Rebase.

🔄 Git Merge
You say: “Let’s bring the main branch changes into my feature branch — and keep track of where they came from.”

✅ Keeps full history
✅ Creates a new merge commit
❌ History becomes a bit messy (but safe)

Real-time use case:
Great when working in teams — everyone’s contributions are preserved visibly.

🔁 Git Rebase
You say: “Let’s pretend my work started from the latest version of main — clean and direct.”

✅ Creates a clean, linear history
✅ No extra merge commits
❌ Rewrites history (be careful if others are using this branch)

Real-time use case:
Great when cleaning up your local commits before creating a pull request.

🧠 When to Use What?

Use merge when collaborating with others — it’s safe and preserves the full story.
Use rebase for polishing your commit history before sharing — but avoid it on shared branches.

💡 Pro Tip: Never rebase a public/shared branch. It will confuse your teammates and mess up syncs.
